/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Think of every small parcel of gas in the cylinder as a point on a map of local equivalence ratio φ against local temperature T.
   - A conventional diesel spray crosses both.
   - Efficiency can still be diesel-like: the engine runs unthrottled at a high compression ratio, and the lean, cool gas loses less heat.
   - HCCI mixes fuel and air before compression, like a petrol engine, and ignites it by compression heat, like a diesel.
   - τ is the ignition delay; A a constant for the fuel and pressure; E/R the activation temperature, about 15 000 K here; T the charge temperature in K.
   - The control levers are all indirect: intake air temperature, the EGR rate, hot residual gas trapped by variable valve timing, the compression ratio and the fuel’s reactivity.
   - No production car runs pure HCCI.
   - PCCI (premixed charge compression ignition) uses one fuel injected early, so it has time to mix before it ignites.
   - RCCI (reactivity controlled compression ignition) uses two fuels of different reactivity.
   - RCCI looks like the dual-fuel engine but works differently: there the diesel pilot lights a flame that travels through the gas; in RCCI the whole charge self-ignites in sequence, from more to less reactive parcels, with no flame front.
*/
